1
00:00:00,070 --> 00:00:02,900
Ever wondered how your fridge keeps your food cold?

2
00:00:03,020 --> 00:00:08,230
Here's the secret: a fridge doesn't make cold. It moves heat out.

3
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:12,640
The trick? When a liquid evaporates, it soaks up the heat around it.

4
00:00:12,620 --> 00:00:17,440
So a special fluid, the refrigerant, flows in a closed loop of pipes.

5
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:21,920
Inside, it evaporates at low pressure and pulls heat out of your food.

6
00:00:21,980 --> 00:00:27,100
The compressor sucks in that vapor and squeezes it. Squeezed gas gets hot!

7
00:00:27,220 --> 00:00:31,710
At the back, the hot gas gives its heat to the kitchen and turns liquid again.

8
00:00:31,750 --> 00:00:35,600
That's why the back of your fridge feels warm: it's your food's heat!

9
00:00:35,670 --> 00:00:40,570
Then a tiny tube drops the pressure. The liquid turns ice cold again.

10
00:00:40,630 --> 00:00:45,290
And the loop restarts: heat in on the inside, heat out at the back.

11
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:49,850
When it's cold enough, the thermostat stops the compressor. Silence!

12
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:54,050
Well done! You now know how a fridge really makes cold.

13
00:00:54,180 --> 00:00:57,760
Cogwise: how things work. Follow for the next one!
